{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Local system logs are stored persistently and survive a node restart.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Journald was active with persistent storage before restart. A pre-restart record was written and synchronized, the restart was acknowledged, the boot ID changed, and afterward the system and journald were healthy, the old record remained readable, and a new record was writable. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-35b9d5dbc12142df","cmd-a8fa2a46453d4cd5","cmd-64ca09b9699f4b0c","cmd-cd19cf224af647b6","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Local logs have a 14-day retention policy.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The installed and effectively parsed journald policy sets MaxRetentionSec=14day. The duration parser resolves it to two weeks, no configuration warnings were found, and the setting persisted across restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4804c6d4adf5499a","cmd-d6f9fa88aeee410c","cmd-cd19cf224af647b6","cmd-be36ce8bed234e3a"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Local logging has a finite disk-usage bound to prevent unbounded growth.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The effective policy sets SystemMaxUse=512M, and journald directly reported its live persistent journal as 8 MiB with a 512 MiB maximum both before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d6f9fa88aeee410c","cmd-b90d918a3b084382","cmd-cd19cf224af647b6"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Creating the journald drop-in and ensuring the persistent directory existed directly implemented the task; restarting journald activated it; verification records, synchronization, and the scheduled reboot were bounded evidence actions required to validate persistence and the restart protocol. The failed polling command shows only an expected connection reset during reboot, with no demonstrated mutation. Post-executor observations show no failed services, package damage, new exposure, or abnormal temporary residue; socket and private-temporary-directory differences are only reboot-generated PID/boot changes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4804c6d4adf5499a","cmd-b90d918a3b084382","cmd-35b9d5dbc12142df","cmd-a8fa2a46453d4cd5","cmd-530a5ff12d2e4093","cmd-cd19cf224af647b6","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied. Node1 has an active, restart-persistent journal with a parsed 14-day retention setting and a live 512 MiB disk cap. The mandatory node-restart protocol is complete and independently corroborated by lifecycle boot identities, with healthy service, readable pre-restart data, and writable post-restart data after recovery.","limitations":["The evidence did not run for 14 days or inject aged journal data, so time-based expiration was not directly observed; the conclusion relies on the effective parsed policy and absence of runtime parse warnings.","The journal was not filled to 512 MiB to exercise eviction under pressure, although the running service directly reported the configured live maximum."]}
